-- title:
--   Theorem 2 — linear TD(0) converges in expected value to the ideal predictions
-- statement:
--   Let $C$ be an absorbing Markov chain with nonterminal states $N$ (nonempty) and terminal states $T$, let $\mu$ be a distribution of starting probabilities on $N$, let $\nu_j$ ($j\in T$) be outcome distributions with finite expected values $\bar z_j=\int z\,d\nu_j(z)$, and let $\{x_i\mid i\in N\}\subset\mathbb R^K$ be linearly independent observation vectors. Assume every nonterminal state has a positive expected number of visits $d_i>0$ in one sequence.
--
--   Then there exists $\varepsilon>0$ such that for every $\alpha$ with $0<\alpha<\varepsilon$, every initial weight vector $w_0$, and every i.i.d. stream of sequences generated by $C$, $\mu$ and $\nu$ on a probability space, the weight vectors $w_n$ of linear TD(0) with updates after each sequence satisfy, for every $i\in N$: each $x_i^\top w_n$ is integrable, and
--
--   $$\lim_{n\to\infty}E\{x_i^\top w_n\}=E\{z\mid i\}=\bigl[(I-Q)^{-1}h\bigr]_i,\qquad [h]_i=\sum_{j\in T}p_{ij}\bar z_j .$$
--
--   This is the first proof that a TD method converges to the correct predictions.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\varepsilon$ is chosen after the chain, $\mu$, $\nu$ and the $x_i$, and before $\alpha$, $w_0$ and the probability space. The hypothesis $d_i>0$ is the paper's discarding of never-visited states (p. 25); without it the theorem fails, since the weight component of an unvisited state never changes. Integrability is asserted so that the expectation is not the default value of a non-integrable function.
-- source:
--   Sutton (1988), Machine Learning 3:9–44, §4.1, Theorem 2, pp. 24–25 (PDF pp. 16–17)

import Definitions.Def_SuttonTD_Convergence_ExpectedVisits
import Definitions.Def_SuttonTD_Convergence_EpisodeStream
import Definitions.Def_SuttonTD_Convergence_LinearTD0
open Filter Topology MeasureTheory Matrix

namespace SuttonTD.Convergence

/-- **Theorem 2** (Sutton 1988, §4.1, pp. 24–25, PDF pp. 16–17): "For any absorbing Markov chain,
for any distribution of starting probabilities `μ_i`, for any outcome distributions with finite
expected values `z̄_j`, and for any linearly independent set of observation vectors
`{x_i | i ∈ N}`, there exists an `ε > 0` such that, for all positive `α < ε` and for any initial
weight vector, the predictions of linear TD(0) (with weight updates after each sequence) converge
in expected value to the ideal predictions (5). That is, if `w_n` denotes the weight vector after
`n` sequences have been experienced, then `lim_{n→∞} E{x_iᵀw_n} = E{z | i} = [(I − Q)⁻¹h]_i`,
`∀ i ∈ N`."

Formalization Note:
* the sequences are i.i.d. draws from the chain started at `μ`, with outcome law `ν_j` at the
  terminal state `j` (`IsEpisodeStream`); `z̄_j = ∫ z dν_j`, assumed finite (`Integrable id`);
* `ε` depends only on the chain, `μ`, `ν` and the `x_i`, and is chosen before `α`, the initial
  weight vector and the probability space;
* the hypothesis `0 < d_i` for all `i` (`d_i` the expected number of visits to `i` in one
  sequence) is the paper's "Each `d_i` is strictly positive, because any state for which
  `d_i = 0` has no probability of being visited and can be discarded" (p. 25); without it the
  theorem is false (an unreachable state's weight never changes);
* `E{x_iᵀw_n}` is a Bochner integral; the conclusion also asserts it is the integral of an
  integrable function, so the limit is not an artefact of the junk value `0`. -/
theorem theorem_2 {N T : Type*} [Fintype N] [DecidableEq N] [Nonempty N] [Fintype T] [Nonempty T]
    (C : AbsorbingChain N T) (μ : N → ℝ) (hμ0 : ∀ i, 0 ≤ μ i) (hμ1 : ∑ i, μ i = 1)
    (ν : T → Measure ℝ) [∀ j, IsProbabilityMeasure (ν j)] (hν : ∀ j, Integrable id (ν j))
    {K : ℕ} (x : N → Fin K → ℝ) (hx : LinearIndependent ℝ x)
    (hd : ∀ i, 0 < C.expectedVisits μ i) :
    ∃ ε > 0, ∀ α : ℝ, 0 < α → α < ε → ∀ (w₀ : Fin K → ℝ)
      {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
      (e : ℕ → Ω → Episode N T), IsEpisodeStream C μ ν P e →
      ∀ i : N, (∀ n, Integrable (fun ω => x i ⬝ᵥ tdWeights x α w₀ e n ω) P) ∧
        Tendsto (fun n => ∫ ω, x i ⬝ᵥ tdWeights x α w₀ e n ω ∂P) atTop
          (𝓝 (((1 - C.Q)⁻¹ *ᵥ C.h (fun j => ∫ z, z ∂ν j)) i)) := by sorry

end SuttonTD.Convergence
